Hook RoleManager so a guest asking "am I the default browser?" does not die.

SYMPTOM
    With patch 0010 Chrome's renderers survive and pages actually commit -- and that is
    exactly what makes this reachable. On the first completed navigation:

        cr_JniAndroid  E  java.lang.SecurityException:
            Package com.android.chrome does not belong to 10235
              at android.app.role.IRoleManager$Stub$Proxy.isRoleHeldAsUser
              at android.app.role.RoleManager.isRoleHeld
              at hgr.didFinishNavigationInPrimaryMainFrame
        chromium  A  [FATAL] Uncaught Java exception in native code ...
        libc      A  Fatal signal 5 (SIGTRAP)

    10235 is the host UID. The exception is thrown on a callback invoked from native code,
    so Chrome's own handler cannot swallow it: the process aborts.

    This is the same signature already recorded for API 37 emulators in commit c94f67c
    ("Package com.android.chrome does not belong to [UID] during IRoleManager
    .isRoleHeldAsUser"). It is not new; it was simply unreachable while navigation never
    completed.

WHY IT HAPPENS
    RoleManager.isRoleHeld(role) invokes
    IRoleManager.isRoleHeldAsUser(roleName, packageName, userId). RoleManagerService lets a
    caller ask about a package it owns and refuses otherwise. In a container the Binder
    calling UID is the host's while the package argument is the guest's.

WHY BCORE DID NOT CATCH IT
    No hook. Bcore has no IRoleManagerProxy and never registered the "role" service, so the
    call went to the real service unchanged -- the same gap patch 0009 found for
    LocaleManager.

THE FIX
    A new IRoleManagerProxy, registered in HookManager beside ILocaleManagerProxy, hooking
    isRoleHeldAsUser and rewriting the package argument to the host package.

    One difference from patches 0006 and 0009 worth stating, because it is the obvious place
    to get this wrong: the package is **args[1]** here, not args[0] -- args[0] is the role
    name. Rewriting index 0 would replace the role with a package name and return a
    confident, meaningless answer rather than crashing, which is worse. The hook checks the
    index it actually writes.

SECURITY RATIONALE
    As patches 0006 and 0009:

    - No UID, package, signature or certificate is spoofed and no SecurityException is
      suppressed. The platform check still runs and passes on its own terms.
    - The platform rule is "the caller must own the package it asks about". In a container
      the caller genuinely IS the host, so asking about the host package is the truthful
      request.
    - userId is untouched, so no other Android user is addressed.
    - The package argument is replaced with the host package, never an arbitrary one, so a
      guest cannot use this to probe a third party's roles.
    - A guest cannot hold a platform role at all -- it is not separately installed -- so the
      host's answer is the only legitimate one available. The practical effect is that a
      cloned browser learns it is not the default browser, which is true of the clone.
    - Play Integrity, SafetyNet, GMS and account/OAuth paths are untouched.

VERIFICATION
    Chrome in a container on the emulator of record, API 35. Before: SIGTRAP on the first
    completed navigation, reproducible. Expected after: no "does not belong to" exception,
    no SIGTRAP, and navigation completes.

